{# Coverage and reconciliation - 3.3.

   Two columns, Declared and Computed, for each of the seven rows. A row where
   the two disagree is marked with an asterisk and stated in words below, since
   2.9 forbids printing one figure without the other and colour alone would not
   survive a photocopy.

   Under the table: every checkpoint the robot did not reach, with the reason it
   recorded, and every checkpoint that captured no evidence, with what the check
   observed. Both lists are built from detect_gaps' output, so they name the
   same checkpoints the manifest does (4.4, 5.4). #}
<section class="section coverage" id="coverage">

  <h2>Coverage and reconciliation</h2>

  <p class="lede">
    Declared is what the record reported. Computed is what its checkpoint array
    actually contains. Where the two differ, both figures are printed; neither
    is preferred over the other.
  </p>

  <table class="reconciliation">
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Row</th>
        <th class="figure">Declared</th>
        <th class="figure">Computed</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {% for row in count_rows %}
        <tr{% if row.disagrees %} class="count-disagrees"{% endif %}>
          <th>{{ row.label }}{% if row.disagrees %} *{% endif %}</th>
          <td class="figure">{{ row.declared | show("Not declared") }}</td>
          <td class="figure">{{ row.computed }}</td>
        </tr>
      {% endfor %}
    </tbody>
  </table>

  {% if counts_disagree %}
    <p class="table-note">* Declared and computed disagree. Each conflict is stated below.</p>
  {% endif %}

  {# The heading prints whether or not there are conflicts. Without the empty
     state a reader cannot tell a run whose counts all agreed from one where the
     list failed to render, and the agreement is a finding of its own. #}
  <h3>Count conflicts</h3>

  {% if count_conflicts %}
    <p class="lede">
      Each row above where the record's declared count and its own data
      disagree, stated in full. Both figures are printed; neither is corrected.
    </p>
    <ul class="conflicts">
      {% for gap in count_conflicts %}
        <li><span class="gap-tag">{{ gap.gap_type }}</span> {{ gap.detail }}</li>
      {% endfor %}
    </ul>
  {% else %}
    <p class="absent-note">
      Every count the record declared agrees with the data it describes. A count
      the record never declared is shown as not declared in the table above.
    </p>
  {% endif %}

  {% if empty_record %}
    {# Neither list below can say anything true about a run with no
       checkpoints: "every checkpoint was reached" is vacuously true and reads
       as a finding. 3.6 asks for a plain statement instead. #}
    <h3>Checkpoints</h3>
    <p class="absent-strong">
      <span class="gap-tag">{{ empty_record.gap_type }}</span>
      No checkpoints were recorded for this run.
    </p>
  {% else %}

    <h3>Checkpoints not reached</h3>
    {% if missed %}
      <table class="data-table">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Checkpoint</th><th>ID</th><th>Reason recorded</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {% for item in missed %}
            <tr>
              <td>{{ item.checkpoint_name }}</td>
              <td>{{ item.checkpoint_id }}</td>
              <td class="absent">
                <span class="gap-tag">{{ item.gap_type }}</span>
                {{ item.detail }}
              </td>
            </tr>
          {% endfor %}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    {% else %}
      <p>Every checkpoint on the route was reached.</p>
    {% endif %}

    <h3>Checkpoints with no evidence</h3>
    {% if no_evidence %}
      <table class="data-table">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Checkpoint</th><th>ID</th><th>Observed</th><th>Why</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {% for item in no_evidence %}
            <tr>
              <td>{{ item.checkpoint_name }}</td>
              <td>{{ item.checkpoint_id }}</td>
              <td>{{ item.observed | show }}</td>
              <td class="absent">
                <span class="gap-tag">{{ item.gap_type }}</span>
                {{ item.detail }}
              </td>
            </tr>
          {% endfor %}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    {% else %}
      <p>Every checkpoint recorded at least one evidence image.</p>
    {% endif %}

  {% endif %}

  {# The evidence count check, stated whether or not it found anything. A check
     that ran and passed silently is indistinguishable, to a reader, from a check
     that never ran, so every checkpoint gets a row. Every figure and every
     sentence here comes from detect_gaps' output (5.4), so a marked row and a
     manifest gap are the same fact. #}
  <h3>Evidence count reconciliation</h3>
  <p class="lede">
    For each checkpoint: the evidence count the event log declared, the number of
    evidence paths the checkpoint listed, and how many of those paths resolved to
    a usable image file. Where the three do not agree the row is marked and the
    reason is printed beside it.
  </p>

  {% if evidence_rows %}
    <table class="data-table evidence-counts">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Checkpoint</th>
          <th class="figure">Declared</th>
          <th class="figure">Listed</th>
          <th class="figure">Resolved</th>
          <th>Result</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {% for row in evidence_rows %}
          <tr{% if row.mismatch %} class="count-disagrees"{% endif %}>
            {# The id under the name rather than beside it: six columns left the
               Result column too narrow for the label it carries, and the id is
               what an auditor matches against the manifest. #}
            <td>
              {{ row.checkpoint_name }}
              <span class="row-id">{{ row.checkpoint_id }}</span>
            </td>
            <td class="figure">{{ row.declared | join(", ") | show("Not declared") }}</td>
            <td class="figure">{{ row.referenced }}</td>
            <td class="figure">{{ row.resolved }}</td>
            <td>
              {% if row.mismatch %}
                {# The gap types this row actually raised, named as the manifest
                   files them: twelve declared against twelve listed with one
                   unreadable file is a MISSING_IMAGE, not a count conflict. #}
                {% for name in row.gap_types %}
                  <span class="gap-tag">{{ name }}</span>
                {% endfor %}
              {% elif row.declared %}
                Reconciled
              {% else %}
                No count declared
              {% endif %}
            </td>
          </tr>
        {% endfor %}
      </tbody>
    </table>

    {# The reasons sit under the table rather than in the Result column: the
       path a resolution failed on is longer than any column on this page, and
       the row above is what says which checkpoint it belongs to. Same shape as
       the count conflicts higher up - mark the row, state the conflict below. #}
    {% if evidence_mismatches %}
      <p class="table-note">
        A marked row names the gaps it raised, under the names the run manifest
        files them: COUNT_MISMATCH where the declared count is not the number of
        paths listed, MISSING_IMAGE where a listed path gave no usable file.
      </p>

      <h4>Why a row did not reconcile</h4>
      <ul class="conflicts">
        {% for row in evidence_mismatches %}
          <li>
            {{ row.checkpoint_name }} ({{ row.checkpoint_id }})
            <ul class="reasons">
              {% for reason in row.reasons %}
                <li>{{ reason }}</li>
              {% endfor %}
            </ul>
          </li>
        {% endfor %}
      </ul>
    {% endif %}
  {% else %}
    <p class="absent-strong">
      No evidence count was declared and no checkpoint listed an evidence image.
    </p>
  {% endif %}

</section>

{# Every gap in one place - C-2.

   The same list the manifest carries in its `gaps` array, in the same order:
   per checkpoint in route order, then the run-level ones. 4.4 requires every
   manifest gap to be visible in the PDF and every PDF gap to be in the
   manifest, and this is where a reader checks that by eye rather than by diff.

   Nothing here is new. Each gap has already been stated where it belongs - in
   a checkpoint's block, in a cell of its evidence grid, under the
   reconciliation table - and this repeats them so they can be counted.

   Its own section, so it starts on a fresh page and the reconciliation table
   above keeps its own. It renders with the coverage section, which is the
   section 5.7 gives it a switch under. #}
<section class="section gap-list" id="gaps">

  <h2>All gaps</h2>

  {% if gap_rows %}
    <p class="lede">
      Every gap this run raised, in the order the run manifest lists them. Each
      appears in full where it belongs as well; this table is for reading the
      whole set at once, and for checking it against the manifest.
    </p>

    <p class="gap-count">
      {{ gap_rows | length }} gap{{ "" if gap_rows | length == 1 else "s" }} recorded.
    </p>

    <table class="data-table gap-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Gap type</th>
          <th>Item</th>
          <th>Detail</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {% for row in gap_rows %}
          <tr>
            <td><span class="gap-tag">{{ row.gap_type }}</span></td>
            <td>
              {{ row.item_id }}
              {% if row.item_name %}
                <span class="row-id">{{ row.item_name }}</span>
              {% elif row.item_id == run_level %}
                <span class="row-id">the run itself</span>
              {% endif %}
            </td>
            <td>{{ row.detail }}</td>
          </tr>
        {% endfor %}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  {% else %}
    <p class="absent-note">
      No gaps were raised for this run. Every checkpoint was reached, every
      image referenced resolved, every sensor reading was usable, and every
      declared count agreed with the data.
    </p>
  {% endif %}

</section>
